---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, DELETION_EMAIL, LEGAL_ENTITY } from '../lib/links';

export const prerender = true;

const updated = '2026-10-13';

// Every usage event the extension sends (docs/TELEMETRY.md).
const events = [
  ['install, update, browser_update', 'When the extension is installed or updated, or Chrome updates: the old and new version.'],
  ['active_daily', 'Once a day while the browser runs: nothing beyond the common fields below.'],
  ['popup_open, popup_close', 'You opened the popup; when it closes, how long it was open.'],
  ['llms_copy', "You copied a site's llms.txt prompt: the hostname, whether the llms.txt was the site's own or prepared by scoop.md, and whether it was copied on opening or with the button."],
  ['copied_animation_shown', 'The "copied to your clipboard" animation played, and whether your system asks for reduced motion.'],
  ['badge_llms_shown', 'Once an hour at most: how many times the toolbar badge showed "1" (the site has an llms.txt) and "2" (an llms.txt and instructions for agents). Two numbers, no sites or pages.'],
  ['settings_change, options_open', 'You changed the theme or usage statistics, or opened the settings page.'],
  ['telemetry_opt_out, telemetry_opt_in', 'You switched usage statistics off or on.'],
  ['error', 'Something failed: where, an error code and a short message with URLs and hostnames removed.'],
];
// Sent only by versions before 0.6.0, which copied the page itself and shared it (still accepted from them).
const olderEvents = [
  ['scoop_start, scoop_result', "A scoop of the page began and how it ended: the hostname (only its registrable domain for a signed-in page), the path of public docs pages, the outcome, the kinds and number of sources, the size, how long it took, and whether the shared library or the docs index had it."],
  ['copy, rebuild', 'The page package reached the clipboard, or was rebuilt from the page: the hostname, the path (public docs pages only) and the size.'],
  ['crawl_request, crawl_wait', "A crawl was requested for a docs site: the hostname and how it went."],
  ['tab_switch, search, copy_overview, copy_search', "Tabs switched, searches made (the query's length, never its text) and results or overviews copied, on indexed sites."],
  ['share_result, vote, report', "Whether a scoop was shared, and votes and reports on shared scoops."],
];
---

<Base title="Privacy" description="What the scoop.md extension reads, what leaves your device, and what the library keeps.">
  <article class="wrap page prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">privacy</p>
    <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="muted">Last updated <time datetime={updated}>{updated}</time></p>

    <p>
      This policy covers the scoop.md Chrome extension, the shared library at <code>api.scoop.md</code>, and this
      website. They are run by <span class="placeholder">{LEGAL_ENTITY}</span> ("we"). Questions go to{' '}
      <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The extension never reads the pages you visit. Opening it asks the library at <code>api.scoop.md</code> for the site's llms.txt, with the page's origin and the first segment of its path only (for example <code>https://app.example.com/docs</code>), never for a page on your computer or a private network.</li>
      <li>To show "1" or "2" on its icon (the site has an llms.txt, and instructions for agents), it compares each tab's site with a list downloaded to your device and with what the library told it for sites you opened scoop.md on (kept on your device for 7 days); that check never leaves your device.</li>
      <li>
        Usage statistics are on by default: anonymous events about how you use the extension, including the hostname of
        the sites you open it on. The extension says so the first time you open it, with a button to turn them off; you
        can also turn them off in its settings ("Send usage statistics").
      </li>
      <li>No accounts, names or emails. Nothing about sites you don't open scoop.md on. No ads, no third-party trackers, and we don't sell data.</li>
      <li>IP addresses are used for rate limiting and to look up your country, and are not stored.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What the extension reads</h2>
    <p>
      When you open scoop.md, the extension reads the active tab's address. It doesn't read the page, other tabs or your
      browsing history. It writes the llms.txt prompt to your clipboard; it never reads your clipboard. A small
      background script reads the address of the tab you switch to, only to set the "1" or "2" on the icon from a list
      on your device; it handles installs and updates and sends queued usage statistics (once an hour at most, and only
      while they're on).
    </p>
    <p>
      If the library doesn't answer, the extension looks for the site's <code>llms.txt</code> itself, directly from that
      site and without your cookies. That request goes to the documentation site, not to us. This is why the extension
      asks for access to all sites: it can't know in advance which site you'll open it on.
    </p>

    <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The prompt on your clipboard.</li>
      <li>
        Your settings, stored in Chrome's extension storage. Usage statistics on or off syncs with your Google account
        if you use Chrome sync; the theme stays on this device.
      </li>
      <li>A random install ID the extension creates on first run. It identifies an installation, not a person. The settings page shows it.</li>
      <li>The list behind the "1" and "2", and what the library answered for sites you opened scoop.md on (7 days).</li>
      <li>Usage events waiting to be sent (at most 500; the oldest are dropped).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What leaves your device</h2>
    <h3>Opening scoop.md: the site's llms.txt</h3>
    <p>
      When you open scoop.md on a web page, the extension sends only the page's origin and the first segment of its path
      (on <code>https://docs.stripe.com/webhooks/quickstart?lang=node</code>, that's <code>https://docs.stripe.com/webhooks</code>)
      to <code>api.scoop.md/v1/llms</code>, which answers with the
      site's llms.txt (or one scoop.md prepared for it) as a short prompt to copy. Never for pages on your computer or a
      private network. If the library doesn't answer, the extension looks for the site's
      llms.txt itself, without your cookies.
    </p>
    <h3>The "1" and "2" on the icon</h3>
    <p>
      Every 6 hours the extension downloads the list of sites with an llms.txt (and which of them also have instructions
      for agents) from <code>api.scoop.md/v1/sites/index</code> (the request says nothing about you or your tabs). The
      site you're on is looked up in that list on your device, along with what the library answered for sites you opened
      scoop.md on in the last 7 days. Pages on your computer or a private network are never looked up.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every request also carries what any web request does: your IP address and your browser's user agent, plus the
      extension's version.
    </p>

    <h2 id="usage-statistics">Usage statistics</h2>
    <p>
      Unless you turn them off, the extension sends anonymous usage events to <code>api.scoop.md/v1/events</code>, in
      batches: when the popup closes, and from its background script. We use them to see who installs and keeps using
      scoop.md, which documentation sites it's used on, what works and what breaks. The first time you open the
      extension it shows a notice with a button to turn them off; until that notice has been shown, events carry no
      hostname.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>What it records</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{events.map(([names, what]) => <tr><td>{names.split(', ').map((n) => <code class="event">{n}</code>)}</td><td>{what}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Versions before 0.6.0, which copied the page itself and could share it, also sent these (we still accept them from those versions):</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>What it records</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{olderEvents.map(([names, what]) => <tr><td>{names.split(', ').map((n) => <code class="event">{n}</code>)}</td><td>{what}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Every event also carries the time, a random ID for that popup session, your install ID, the extension version, the
      browser's name and version, the operating system and CPU architecture, the browser's interface language and your
      time zone.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Sites</strong>: the hostname of the site you opened scoop.md on, never a path. Nothing at all about pages
        on your computer or a private network (<code>localhost</code>, private IP addresses, <code>.local</code>,
        <code>.internal</code>, <code>.corp</code>, <code>.onion</code> and similar).
      </li>
      <li><strong>Never</strong>: page content, page titles, what you copy, cookies, your name, email or Google account, or anything about tabs you didn't open scoop.md on.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Worked out on our server</strong>: your country, region and continent, and the name of your network
        provider, looked up by Cloudflare from your IP address as the request arrives; the browser family from the user
        agent; and, to spot abuse, a hash of your IP address made with a random key that is created each day and deleted
        after two days. Once a day's key is gone, nobody, including us, can tell which IP address a hash came from or
        link it to another day's. The hashes are deleted after 30 days and never archived. The IP address itself is
        never stored.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Turning it off</strong>: switch off "Send usage statistics" in the extension's settings. The extension
        then sends one last event saying you switched it off, deletes any events still waiting, and sends nothing more
        (not even the daily ping) until you switch it back on. Its requests to the library are marked so they aren't
        recorded as library requests either (below).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Uninstalling</strong>: Chrome opens <code>scoop.md/bye</code>. While usage statistics are on, its address
        includes your install ID and version after a <code>#</code>, the part a browser never sends to a website's
        servers, and the page records the uninstall and, if you click one, your reason.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Library requests</h3>
    <p>
      For lookups to the library (including the extension's llms.txt lookup), the library records the route, the
      hostname, whether anything was found, how long it took, the <code>X-Scoop-Client</code> value and the country, with the time rounded to the hour. Not the IP address,
      region, network, browser or an install ID, and nothing at all for an extension with usage statistics off.
    </p>

    <h2>What the library keeps</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Shared scoops</strong> (from versions before 0.6.0; 0.6.0 shares nothing): the documentation text, source URLs, page address and title, related links, the
        time it was shared and the sharer's install ID. Scoops and their vote counts are public, on this website and
        through the API. Install IDs are never published.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Votes</strong>: scoop ID, install ID, the vote and when it was cast. One per install; a new vote replaces the old one.</li>
      <li><strong>Reports</strong>: as submitted, until they are resolved and for our records afterwards.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Crawl requests</strong>: the page address you asked from, the site, a keyed hash of your install ID
        (requests from one install can be grouped, but the hash can't be turned back into the ID), what the request led
        to and when. Kept for 90 days, then deleted. The site's crawled documentation is kept like any other.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Request logs</strong>: method, route, status, duration and extension version. No IP addresses, install
        IDs or content.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Usage statistics and website page views</strong>: each event in our database for 180 days, then deleted.
        Daily counts (per day, event, hostname, country and version, with no install IDs) are kept. Every event (without
        the IP hash) is also kept in a compressed archive for later analysis, for 13 months.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      IP addresses are used only to apply rate limits and look up the country, and are not stored. Scoops stay until they
      are taken down. When one is taken down, its content is deleted and a record that it existed is kept for audit.
    </p>
    <p>
      We run on Cloudflare, which processes requests to this website and the library on our behalf.
    </p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      scoop.md sets no cookies and loads no third-party scripts. Fonts are served from this site. The library pages show
      only the public data described above.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each page view sends a small event to <code>api.scoop.md</code>: the page's path, the referring site's hostname,
      any <code>utm_source</code>, <code>utm_medium</code> and <code>utm_campaign</code>, and clicks on the main buttons.
      Our server adds the country and the browser family, and counts visitors with a hash of your IP address and user
      agent made with that day's random key (deleted after two days), so a visitor can't be followed from one day to the
      next. If your browser sends Global
      Privacy Control or Do Not Track, no page views or clicks are recorded. The uninstall page is described under
      <a href="#usage-statistics">Usage statistics</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Your choices</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Turn sharing off in the extension's options. Scooping still works; nothing is uploaded and no crawl is requested. For a public page on a site whose docs are indexed, the page's address is still sent to look it up in the index.</li>
      <li>Turn usage statistics off in the extension's options. Scooping and sharing still work.</li>
      <li>Uninstalling the extension deletes its settings and install ID from your browser.</li>
      <li>
        To have the usage statistics linked to your install ID deleted, email <a href={`mailto:${DELETION_EMAIL}`}>{DELETION_EMAIL}</a>
        with the install ID shown on the extension's settings page. We delete its events from our database, stop accepting
        new ones for it, and leave it out of every export of the archive until the archive copy is deleted (13 months after
        it was written). Daily counts don't contain install IDs and stay.
        For shared scoops, votes and reports, email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Takedowns</h2>
    <p>
      If a scoop contains something it shouldn't (your content, wrong or harmful material), use the report button in
      the extension or email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> with the site and scoop. Publishers
      can also ask us to stop storing scoops for their site.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>scoop.md is a developer tool and isn't directed at children.</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If we change what's collected, we'll update this page and its date before the change ships. Material changes will
      be noted in the extension's release notes.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="placeholder">{LEGAL_ENTITY}</span>, <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 1rem 0;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  th,
  td {
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem 0.5rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .event {
    display: block;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .page {
    padding-top: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 1.5rem + 4vw, 4rem);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }
</style>
